    <h1>CAPITALIZATION AND PUNCTUATION</h1>

    <p>This section covers the fundamental rules of capitalization and punctuation used in the English language.</p>

    <h2>1. Capitalization</h2>

    <h3>1.1 Proper Nouns</h3>
    <p>The names of specific people, places, and things are capitalized:</p>
    <p class="indent">• Abraham Lincoln</p>
    <p class="indent">• Mount Everest</p>
    <p class="indent">• Microsoft Corporation</p>
    <p class="indent">• Paris</p>

    <h3>1.2 The First Word of a Sentence</h3>
    <p>Every sentence begins with a capital letter:</p>
    <p class="indent">• The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.</p>

    <h3>1.3 Days, Months, and Holidays</h3>
    <p>Capitalize the names of days, months, and holidays:</p>
    <p class="indent">• Monday, July 4th, Christmas Day</p>

    <h3>1.4 Titles and Honorifics</h3>
    <p>Capitalize titles when they precede a person’s name:</p>
    <p class="indent">• Dr. Martin Luther King Jr.</p>
    <p class="indent">• President Joe Biden</p>
    <p class="indent">• Senator Kamala Harris</p>

    <h2>2. Punctuation</h2>

    <h3>2.1 Periods (Full Stops)</h3>
    <p>Used at the end of declarative sentences and after abbreviations:</p>
    <p class="indent">• The meeting is at 3:00 p.m.</p>

    <h3>2.2 Question Marks</h3>
    <p>Used at the end of interrogative sentences:</p>
    <p class="indent">• Is that the truth?</p>

    <h3>2.3 Exclamation Points</h3>
    <p>Used at the end of exclamatory sentences:</p>
    <p class="indent">• What a beautiful day!</p>

    <h3>2.4 Commas</h3>
    <p>Used to separate items in a list, independent clauses, and nonessential elements:</p>
    <p class="indent">• I like apples, oranges, and bananas.</p>
    <p class="indent">• When you arrive, we will begin.</p>

    <h3>2.5 Semicolons</h3>
    <p>Used to join closely related independent clauses:</p>
    <p class="indent">• I love to read; you prefer television.</p>

    <h3>2.6 Colons</h3>
    <p>Used to introduce a list or explanation:</p>
    <p class="indent">• Bring the following items: pencils, paper, and erasers.</p>

    <h3>2.7 Apostrophes</h3>
    <p>Used for contractions and possession:</p>
    <p class="indent">• It’s a beautiful day. (contraction)</p>
    <p class="indent">• The dog’s tail is wagging. (possession)</p>

    <h3>2.8 Quotation Marks</h3>
    <p>Used to enclose direct quotations:</p>
    <p class="indent">• She said, “I love you.”</p>

    <h3>2.9 Dashes and Parentheses</h3>
    <p>Used for emphasis and asides:</p>
    <p class="indent">• The meeting will be held—please be on time.</p>
    <p class="indent">• (See page 45 for details.)</p>

    <div class="grid">
